structured_output.generality:
This helps equi-joins on a single integer key whose build side is a dimension with dense keys (surrogate keys, FK targets such as part, customer or date dimensions) when the build has more than 64k rows. These builds now finish in parallel instead of in a serial step that grows with the build size. INNER joins with a 1M–4M key range at ≥50% density now use the perfect hash join instead of a regular hash join plus bloom filter; RIGHT joins already qualified and now also build in parallel.

It does not apply to:
- builds under 64k rows, which keep the serial path;
- sparse builds (below 50% density, or a range over 4M);
- multi-condition joins, joins with nested payload types, and joins that spill to disk (that path resets the perfect executor as before).

Possible downside: INNER builds at 50–99% density lose the bloom filter at the probe-side scan, because the perfect join only pushes min/max. Probe rows inside the range with no match then reach the perfect probe, which drops them cheaply. If a duplicate key is found during the parallel build, the join falls back to the regular hash join, with the join filters created as before.
